Consider the following Java code snippet:
```java
List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
names.add("Alice");
// names.add(123); // Line X
```
If uncommented, `Line X` would cause a compile-time error. What is the primary benefit of generics in Java that prevents such an error at runtime?

Given the following class hierarchy:
```python
class A:
    def method(self):
        return "A"

class B(A):
    def method(self):
        return "B"

class C(A):
    def method(self):
        return "C"

class D(B, C):
    pass

obj = D()
print(obj.method())
```
What will be printed to the console when `obj.method()` is called?
